In this lesson, we'll look at what makes a number irrational. A number is rational if you can write it as a fraction of two integers, like three halves or seven over four. When you turn a rational number into a decimal, it either stops at some point, like zero point seven five, or it settles into a repeating pattern, like zero point three three three, forever. An irrational number is just the opposite. It's a real number that cannot be written as that kind of fraction, so its decimal goes on forever without ever repeating. Here's the quick test. Ask yourself, can I write this as a fraction of two integers? If yes, it's rational. If no, it's irrational. You've probably seen the classics, like the square root of two, the square root of five, and pi. Their decimals just keep going with no repeating block. But watch out, a long decimal isn't the same as an irrational one. The giveaway is whether the digits ever settle into a repeating chunk. If they never do, it's irrational. Now let's see it in action.